<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyar Mudar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diyar Mudar is the medieval Arabic name for the western part of the Jazira, the upper Mesopotamian region along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikh-river">Balikh</a>, named after the Mudar tribes settled there in early Islamic times. Its main towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, Edessa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Saruj</a>. In the 10th and 11th centuries it was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>, the Numayrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>.</p></div>
